[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Modelled, not excluded
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two pieces of the C's undefined behaviour turned out to be *observable* and are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reproduced deliberately (see the long comments in `src/lib.rs`):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`cp_dynamic`'s stack frame.** The repeat opcodes overflow `lens[288+32]`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   into `lenlens`, `sym`, `nlen`, `ndst`, `nlit`, the three loop counters and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   finally `n`. Rust reproduces gcc's frame layout (verified against
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `objdump -d`) so the aliasing matches, including gcc's ordering of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `lens[n++] = sym` (the increment is stored *before* the byte write).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `lens[-1]`, read by opcode 16 when `n == 0`, aliases the top byte of the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   spilled `cp_state_t *` — zero for any canonical x86-64 address.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`cp_decode`'s `tree[-1]`.** `cp_state_t` is `#[repr(C)]` with the original
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   field order, so the read lands on the same neighbouring member as in C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`lookup` for `lit`, `lit` for `dst`, `dst` for `len`), and the tree pointers
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   are derived from the struct base so the read keeps valid provenance.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Also: `32 - (key & 0xF)` can be 32, which gcc compiles to `shr %cl` with the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   count masked to 5 bits; Rust reproduces that with `& 31`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cp_build`'s `counts[16]` overflow (indexed by a code length that may exceed 15)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m needs no frame model: whenever it happens, the `assert(len < 16)` in the second
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m loop aborts, and the overflowing stores can only reach `first`, `codes` and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m loop counters — never `sym_count` (at `-0xfc`, *below* `counts` at `-0xe0`), so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the second loop always runs its full range and always aborts. Both libraries
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m therefore end in `SIGABRT` (confirmed by `errors.rs::row15_...`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Test-harness notes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Every call runs in a `fork()`ed child, so `abort()` is a comparable outcome.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Fatal signals are turned into `_exit(128 + signo)` by a handler in the child
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   because this host pipes `core_pattern` to `systemd-coredump` (~380 ms per
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   abort, not suppressed by `RLIMIT_CORE = 0`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * A 40 ms `ITIMER_REAL` watchdog makes non-termination (ERRORS.md row 25) an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   observable, comparable outcome.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Both buffers get 64 KiB zeroed guard regions, because `cp_stored`'s `memcpy`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is bounded by neither. The out guard is compared via an FNV-1a hash so a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   stray write out there is still caught without shipping 64 KiB per case.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Both `.so`s are loaded with `RTLD_NOW | RTLD_LOCAL`, so their identically
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   named globals stay separate and every PLT entry is bound before the fork.
